VERDICT (LANE B breadth worker bKOE, 24 Sept 2026): target best periodic-IC (P=17, 0.0468) and Kasiski
repeat-trigram count (2 pairs total across all 5 messages, no period concentration) both sit at the flat/uniform
level, matched against the same method run on the same-length, same-alphabet German plaintext under a matched
Vigenere control (control_a, true period known: coset IC 0.065-0.081 at the true period, Kasiski distance pairs
12-37 concentrated on the true period and its divisors, seeds 1-3, periods 6/29/9) and under a matched one-time-key
control (control_b: coset IC 0.044-0.047, no period concentration, matching the target and uniform ~0.0385).
Read: this method would recover a real period-2-30 Vigenere-family key on ciphertext this short and this design
(five messages, 924 letters pooled, restart-per-message coset test); it finds none in the target. Combined with
the flat pooled IC (cheap test 1, koehler_ic.out, 0.0399 vs German ~0.076 / English ~0.066 / uniform 0.0385), the
target is not a fixed short-period Vigenere/Beaufort cipher over these five messages; consistent with a one-time
key, a code/book system, or a period outside 2-30. This rules out cheap_tests_in_order item 4 (Vigenere hill-climb)
as the next step; items 2/3 (archive lookup) or 5 (book cipher) remain live per the spec.

